# Principal component analysis

The source standardises all thirteen chemical measurements and applies principal component analysis. This prevents measurements such as V14, whose raw variance is very large, from dominating solely because of their units.

```biolang
fn read_source_wine(path) {
    let data = read_csv(path, {header: false})
    let names = colnames(data)
    data = rename(data, names[0], "V1")
    data = rename(data, names[1], "V2")
    data = rename(data, names[2], "V3")
    data = rename(data, names[3], "V4")
    data = rename(data, names[4], "V5")
    data = rename(data, names[5], "V6")
    data = rename(data, names[6], "V7")
    data = rename(data, names[7], "V8")
    data = rename(data, names[8], "V9")
    data = rename(data, names[9], "V10")
    data = rename(data, names[10], "V11")
    data = rename(data, names[11], "V12")
    data = rename(data, names[12], "V13")
    rename(data, names[13], "V14")
}

let wine = read_source_wine("data/wine.data")
let standardised = wine |> select("V2", "V3", "V4", "V5", "V6", "V7", "V8", "V9", "V10", "V11", "V12", "V13", "V14")
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V2", |row| (row.V2 - mean(wine |> col("V2"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V2")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V3", |row| (row.V3 - mean(wine |> col("V3"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V3")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V4", |row| (row.V4 - mean(wine |> col("V4"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V4")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V5", |row| (row.V5 - mean(wine |> col("V5"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V5")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V6", |row| (row.V6 - mean(wine |> col("V6"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V6")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V7", |row| (row.V7 - mean(wine |> col("V7"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V7")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V8", |row| (row.V8 - mean(wine |> col("V8"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V8")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V9", |row| (row.V9 - mean(wine |> col("V9"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V9")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V10", |row| (row.V10 - mean(wine |> col("V10"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V10")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V11", |row| (row.V11 - mean(wine |> col("V11"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V11")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V12", |row| (row.V12 - mean(wine |> col("V12"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V12")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V13", |row| (row.V13 - mean(wine |> col("V13"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V13")))
standardised = standardised |> mutate("V14", |row| (row.V14 - mean(wine |> col("V14"))) / stdev(wine |> col("V14")))

let wine_pca = pca(standardised, 13)
table({
    component: ["PC1", "PC2", "PC3", "PC4", "PC5"],
    variance: wine_pca.explained_variance |> head(5),
    proportion: wine_pca.explained_variance_ratio |> head(5)
})
```

The source reports component variances 4.7058503, 2.4969737, and 1.4460720 for PC1-PC3. They explain 36.2%, 19.2%, and 11.1% of the total variance. Because the thirteen standardised variables each have variance one, total variance is 13.

## How many components?

![Canonical scree plot](figures/source/image6.png)

The source gives three complementary decisions:

- The scree plot bends at component 4, suggesting retention of PC1-PC3.
- Kaiser's rule also retains PC1-PC3 because only their variances exceed one.
- An 80% cumulative-variance threshold retains PC1-PC5, which explain 80.2%.

## Loadings and scores

```biolang
table({
    variable: ["V2", "V3", "V4", "V5", "V6", "V7", "V8", "V9", "V10", "V11", "V12", "V13", "V14"],
    PC1_loading: wine_pca.loadings |> map(|loadings| loadings[0]),
    PC2_loading: wine_pca.loadings |> map(|loadings| loadings[1])
})
```

For PC1, the strongest absolute loadings include V8, V7, V13, V10, V12, and V14 on one side, and V9, V3, and V5 on the other. PC2 is driven especially by V11 and V2 in contrast with V12. These match the source loadings to its printed precision. As with every eigendecomposition, an entire component may be multiplied by -1 without changing the result.

![Canonical scatterplot of the first two principal components](figures/source/image7.png)

The canonical plot uses the source orientation: cultivar 1 is low on PC1, cultivar 3 is high on PC1, and cultivar 2 is high on PC2.

```biolang
assert abs(wine_pca.explained_variance[0] - 4.7058503) < 0.0000001, "source PC1 variance"
assert abs(wine_pca.explained_variance[1] - 2.4969737) < 0.0000001, "source PC2 variance"
assert abs(wine_pca.explained_variance[2] - 1.4460720) < 0.0000001, "source PC3 variance"
assert abs(wine_pca.explained_variance_ratio[0] - 0.36198848) < 0.0000001, "source PC1 proportion"
assert abs(abs(wine_pca.loadings[0][0]) - 0.144329395) < 0.0000001, "source V2 PC1 loading up to arbitrary sign"
assert abs(abs(wine_pca.loadings[6][0]) - 0.422934297) < 0.0000001, "source V8 PC1 loading up to arbitrary sign"
assert abs(abs(wine_pca.scores[0][0]) - 3.30742097) < 0.0000001, "source first PC1 score up to arbitrary sign"
"PCA eigenvalues, loadings, scores, and two canonical figures verified."
```

